## a b s t r a c t
The construction of tightly integrated heterostructures with metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) has been confirmed to be an effective way for improved hydrogen evolution. However, the reported tightly integrated MOF/COF hybrids were usually limited to the covalent connection of COFs with aldehyde groups and ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -MOF via Schiff base reaction, restricting the development of MOF/COF hybrids. Herein, a covalent triazine framework (CTF-1), a subtype of crystalline COFs, was integrated with a conductive two-dimensional (2D) MOF (Ni-CAT-1) by a novel coordinating connection mode for significantly enhanced visible-light-driven hydrogen evolution. The terminal amidine groups in the CTF-1 layers offer dual N sites for the coordination of metal ions, which provides the potential of coordinating connection between CTF-1 and Ni-CAT-1. The conductive 2D Ni-CAT-1 in Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 hybrids effectively facilitates the separation of photogenerated carriers of CTF-1 component, and the resultant hybrid materials show significantly enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity. In particular, the Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 (1:19) sample exhibits the maximum hydrogen evolution rate of 8.03 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ , which is about four times higher than that of the parent CTF-1 $(1.96{\mathrm{~mmol~g}}^{-1}{\mathrm{h}}^{-1})$ . The enhanced photocatalytic activity of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 is mainly attributed to the incorporation of conductive MOF which leads to the formation of a Z-Scheme heterostructure, promoting the electron transfer in hybrid materials. The coordinating combination mode of Ni-CAT-1 and CTF-1 in this work provides a novel strategy for constructing tightly integrated MOF/COF hybrid materials. $\circledcirc$ 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
## 1. Introduction
With the expansion of human activities, the energy crisis has become an increasingly serious concern because of the excessive consumption of fossil fuels [1–4]. Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution is a promising endeavor that has the potential to ensure sustainable energy security and address several global challenges, such as environmental protection and climate change [5–8]. In the last few decades, many photocatalysts, including inorganic and organic materials, have been reported successively with the purpose for efficient hydrogen evolution [9–13]. Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) with predictable lattice structures have become a frontier study in the material sciences, and are undergoing rapid progress for many promising applications [14–16]. In particular, the uniform porous structure of COFs endows them with potential photoelectric applications [17,18]. As a vital subtype of COFs, the covalent triazine frameworks (CTFs) which are comprised of nitrogen-rich triazine and aromatic rings as linking units have attracted much attention due to their intrinsically high chemical and thermal stability as well as a fully $\pi$ -conjugated structure [19–21]. However, the rapid recombination of photogenerated electrons and holes still limits the photocatalytic activity of a single CTF. Constructing a heterostructure is considered as an effective means to inhibit the recombination of photogenerated electrons and holes through interface interaction [22–26].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), another type of porous crystalline materials, have similar advantages to COFs [27–30]. Recently, an emerging subclass of the MOFs, conductive MOFs (a group of graphene-like two-dimensional (2D) MOFs), have been increasingly studied, owning to their electrical conductivities and periodic porous structures. In particular, the triphenylene-fused metal catecholate-based conductive MOFs (M CAT) with p-d 2D structures possess the merits of a large specific surface area and high electrical conductivity [31]. The integration of MOFs and COFs can maximumly inherit the porous features of parent materials, which significantly contributes to the photocatalytic reaction by facilitating the mass transfer. Therefore, MOFs are considered as a suitable hybrid specie for constructing COF-based heterostructures. Previously, our group first put forward the rational design of covalently integrated MOF/COF hybrid materials for enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution [32]. In detail, with $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ in $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -UiO-66 as the grafting site, TpPa-1-COF was grown in situ on $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -UiO-66 through the Schiff base reaction. The resulting $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -UiO-66/TpPa1-COF demonstrates the best performance among various COF- or MOF-based photocatalysts at the time. After this, a lot of work about covalently integrated MOF/COF composites with intrinsic – $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ in MOFs as the grafting site was published by researchers, such as Ti-MOF $@$ DM-LZU1, UiO-66- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}\boldsymbol{@}^{\prime}$ TpMA, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL- $125@$ TAPBPDA and $\mathsf{M I L-}101\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{TpM}$ A [33–35]. In above work, the $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ groups in MOF components seem to be inevitable in constructing covalently integrated MOF/COF hybrid materials. Very recently, we developed a new strategy for constructing covalently integrated MOF/COF hybrid materials by the post-synthetic modification of metal nodes in MOFs without $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ in the ligands, successfully obtaining the core–shell MOF-808@TpPa-1-COF connected by covalent bonds [36]. The significant role of a tight combination between COFs and MOFs via covalent bond connection for the separation and migration of photogenerated charge has been fully confirmed (Scheme 1a). It is expected that apart from the covalent bond connection, the other strong chemical bond connection between MOFs and COFs can also promote the transfer of electronics at the interface. Especially, the tight integration of 2D conductive MOFs with photoactive COFs can effectively facilitate the separation of photogenerated carriers of COF components because conductive MOFs own spatially overlapping ligands, usually resulting in the formation of continuous electronic bands to mediate through-space charge transfer [37]. Therefore, constructing tightly integrated conductive MOF/CTF heterostructures by coordination bonds possesses an excellent potential to promote photocatalytic hydrogen evolution.
In this work, we chose a typical covalent triazine framework (CTF-1) as a model photocatalyst at whose edge there are abundant coordinately unsaturated N sites [38]. In order to avoid extensive recombination of charges on the surface of CTF-1, a conductive MOF (Ni-CAT-1) [39] as hybrid specie was introduced to fabricate MOF/CTF heterostructure where Ni-CAT-1 was grafted at coordinately unsaturated N sites of CTF-1 edge by Ni-N coordination bonds. It was confirmed that the MOF/CTF heterostructure was fabricated successfully. Moreover, Ni-CAT-1 and CTF-1 are tightly combined in the as-obtained Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1hybrid materials. The Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1hybrid materials show significantly enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity relative to the parent CTF-1. In particular, Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 (1:19) sample exhibits an $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of $8.03\mathrm{\mmol}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ , which is about four times higher than that of the pristine CTF-1 $\mathrm{^{\prime}1.96~m m o l~g^{-1}h^{-1}};$ . The enhanced photocatalytic activity of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1hybrid materials is attributed to the coordination bond connection which promotes the electron transfer from CTF-1 to Ni-CAT-1 in hybrid materials. Hence, this work provides a versatile molecularmaterial platform for constructing tightly integrated heterostructure in photocatalysis.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials and characterization
2,3,6,7,10,11 hexahydroxytriphenylene was purchased from Jilin Chinese Academy of Sciences-Yanshen Technology Co., ltd. Ni $(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , benzene-1,4-dicarbonitrile, tetrahydrofuran, 1,4- Benzenedimethanol, terephthalamidine, cesium carbonate and dimethyl sulfoxide were purchased from Aladdin Industrial Corporation. All of the reagents were analytically pure and used without further purification. The composition, structures and texture properties of materials were investigated by X-ray powder diffraction patterns (XRD, Bruker D8 X-ray diffractometer), Fourier transform infrared spectra (FT-IR, Spectrum 100), transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-2100 electron microscope), scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi S-4800), thermogravimetric analyses (TGA, SDTA851e). $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms were determined by Micromeritics ASAP 2020 analyzer at 77 K. Before the measurement, the samples were degassed at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . The surface chemical state was studied by $\mathsf{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ESCALAB 250). Optical properties were also analyzed by UV– vis diffuse-reflectance spectra (UV–vis DRS, Lambda 35 spectrometer) and photoluminescence spectra (PL, SPEX Fluorolog-3 spectrofluorometer with an excitation wavelength of $350~\mathrm{nm}$ ). Time resolved PL of the samples was recorded with FLS1000 Photoluminescence Spectrometer under excitation of a nanosecond flashlamp.
The electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS), Mott-Schottky plots and photocurrent-time (I-T) profiles were recorded on the CHI660E electrochemical workstation with a standard three-electrode system with the photocatalyst-coated fluorine doped tin oxide (FTO) conductive glass as the working electrode, Pt plate as the counter electrode, and an $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ electrode as the reference electrode. The $0.25\mathrm{~M~}$ of ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ solution was used as the electrolyte. The assynthesized sample $\cdot2\mathrm{mg},$ ) was added to the mixed solution of ethanol (1 mL) and Nafion $10\upmu\mathrm{L})$ . The working electrodes were prepared by dropping the suspension $(200~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ onto an FTO glass substrate electrode surface. A 300 W Xenon lamp with a $420\mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter was used as the light source during the measurement.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Ni-CAT-1
Ni-CAT-1 was synthesized according to a previous report [39]. The solid mixture of 2,3,6,7,10,11 hexahydroxytriphenylene $(7~\mathrm{mg})$ and ${\mathrm{Ni}}(0\mathsf{A c})_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ ( $\mathrm{10~mg)}$ was dissolved in deionized water $(4.0\ \mathrm{mL})$ ), and the as-obtained solution was placed in a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ glass vial. The reaction mixture was heated in an isothermal oven at $85~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The reaction mixture was allowed to cool naturally to room temperature, and the crystals were washed with deionized water $(3\times5~\mathrm{mL}$ and acetone $(3\times5~\mathrm{mL}$ ). After drying in an oven at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the product obtained is Ni-CAT-1.
## 2.3. Synthesis of terephthalamidine
The terephthalamidine was synthesized according to the previous reports [40,41]. The solution of benzene-1,4-dicarbonitrile $(1.28\mathrm{~g},8.19\mathrm{~mmol})$ in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of tetrahydrofuran, and $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of 1 M LiN $(\mathrm{SiMe}_{3})_{2}$ solution was added dropwise in $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ at $0~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The mixture was stirred at $25^{\circ}C$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and then cooled to $0\ {}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ The reaction was quenched by carefully adding 6 M HCl-EtOH (1:1, $40~\mathrm{mL}$ . The mixture was set aside overnight. The white precipitates were filtered, washed with ethyl acetate and tetrahydrofuran, and then recrystallized from the $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ -EtOH mixture.
## 2.4. Synthesis of CTF-1
1,4-Benzenedimethanol $\left(46\mathrm{mg},0.33\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ ), terephthalamidine $\left\langle108\mathrm{\mg},0.66\mathrm{\mmol}\right\rangle$ , and cesium carbonate $(232~\mathrm{mg},0.71~\mathrm{mmol})$ 1 were added to the dimethyl sulfoxide $(10.0\mathrm{mL})$ in a round bottom flask ( $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ . The mixture was heated at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , then up to $180^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The precipitates of the yellow suspension were washed with dilute hydrochloric acid $1\mathrm{~M},3\times10\mathrm{~mL}$ to remove residual cesium carbonate, and further washed with water $(3\ \times\ 10\ \mathrm{\mL})$ , ethanol $(3\ \times\ 10\ \mathrm{\mL})$ and tetrahydrofuran $(3\times10~\mathrm{mL})$ . The filtered solids were vacuum-dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ for 8 h to obtain CTF-1.
## 2.5. Synthesis of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1
The solid mixture of 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexahydroxytriphenylene, Ni $(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and CTF-1 was dissolved in deionized water $(5.0\mathrm{mL})$ 1 in a $10\ \mathrm{mL}$ glass vial. The reaction mixture was heated in an isothermal oven at $85~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The reaction mixture was allowed to cool naturally to room temperature. The crystals were washed with deionized water $(3~\times~5~\mathrm{\mL})$ and then acetone $(3\times5~\mathrm{{mL}}$ . After drying in an oven at $60^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the product obtained is Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1. The additive amount of CTF-1, Ni $(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and 2,3,6,7,10,11 hexahydroxytriphenylene is shown in Table S1.
## 2.6. Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution
The photocatalytic performance of samples was evaluated by the water splitting for hydrogen evolution in a $500~\mathrm{mL}$ Pyrex reaction vessel. The 300 W Xenon arc lamp with a cut-off filter $(\lambda>420\mathrm{nm})$ was used as the visible light source. In a typical experiment, $10\mathrm{mg}$ of sample was suspended in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of PBS buffer solution (0.1 M, $\mathrm{PH}=7\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) by sonication for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . Afterwards, $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of sodium ascorbate (SA) and $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of chloroplatinic acid solution were dispersed in the above suspension. Then the mixed solution was irradiated by xenon lamp for $0.5\mathrm{h}$ to promote photodeposition of platinum ion in the solution. The reaction solution was stirred and illuminated under visible light for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The hydrogen evolved was determined by a GC112A gas chromatograph.
## 3. Results and discussion
The in-situ binding method was employed to prepare the coordinately integrated Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 hybrid materials in this work, and the schematic diagram is illustrated in Scheme 1b. CTF-1 was synthesized on the basis of a previous report [40], then the prepared CTF-1 acting as a precursor was added in the synthetic procedure of Ni-CAT-1. In this process, the nickel-oxygen cluster gradually generates around the N sites of CTF-1 margin owning to the coordinating interaction. Thus, Ni-CAT-1 grows in situ on the CTF-1. Finally, a series of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 hybrid materials were obtained by adjusting the proportion of raw materials.
The morphology of as-prepared samples was characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and high resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM). From Fig. 1a, b and d, the CTF-1 presents a flaky accumulation state, while Ni-CAT-1 has a rod-like morphology. Moreover, it is observed that the rod-like Ni-CAT-1 grows on the flake-like CTF-1 substrate (Fig. 1c). As shown in the elemental mapping images (Fig. 1f and S1), the C, O, N and Ni elements are evenly distributed on the surface of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1. In order to further analyze the element distribution in Ni-CAT- $\cdot1/$ CTF-1 sample, the energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) was carried out. It is clear that the mass fraction of C, N, O and Ni elements in Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 is $77.9\%$ , $15.8\ \%$ , $5.8\%$ and $0.5\ \%$ , respectively (Figure S2). Moreover, the HRTEM image has further confirmed the junction state of Ni-CAT-1 and CTF-1 in the NiCAT-1/CTF-1 composite. As exhibited in Fig. 1e, the Ni-CAT-1 with a rod-like structure can be observed on the surface of CTF-1 with a flaky accumulation structure, indicating that the Ni-CAT-1 is grown on the CTF-1 substrate in the in-situ preparation process, which demonstrates that the Ni-CAT-1 combines well with CTF1. Besides, the lattice fringes of Ni-CAT-1 can be clearly observed, and the fringe gap is $1.90~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , corresponding to the (01 0) crystal plane in Ni-CAT-1 (Fig. 1d) [42].
The crystal and chemical structures of as-prepared samples were determined by the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis and Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy. As displayed in Fig. 2a, the diffraction peaks of the as-prepared Ni-CAT-1/CTF1 at $2\uptheta=7.3^{\circ}$ and $26.1^{\circ}$ are assigned to the (100) and (001) planes of CTF-1, while the diffraction peaks at $2\uptheta=4.6^{\circ}$ and $13.9^{\circ}$ can correspond to the (010) and (121) planes of Ni-CAT-1, which indicates the successful preparation of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 hybrid material [43,44]. Besides, a series of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 hybrid materials with different proportions were synthesized by adjusting the dosage of Ni-CAT-1 (Figure S3). It is evident that the diffraction peak of Ni-CAT-1 at $2\uptheta=4.6^{\circ}$ is also gradually sharp when the mass fraction of Ni-CAT-1increases from $5\%$ to $9\%$ . The FT-IR spectra of as-prepared samples are shown in Fig. 2b. The absorption peaks of Ni-CAT-1 at $1462~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1220~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can correspond to the stretching vibration peaks of $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ and $\mathsf{C}{\mathsf{-}}0$ , respectively [45]. The two absorption peaks of CTF-1 at $1308~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1519~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can correspond to $\mathsf{C-N}$ and $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ in the triazine unit, respectively [46]. The peak positions of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 match well with the corresponding stretching vibration peaks of CTF-1. However, due to the coincidence of the peak positions of Ni-CAT-1 and CTF-1, the characteristic peaks of Ni-CAT-1 are not obvious in the composite.
The thermostability of as-prepared samples was investigated by thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) under air atmosphere. As shown in Fig. 2c, compared with Ni-CAT-1, the CTF-1 sample exhibits high thermostability, which is probably attributed to covalent bonding between the two constituent units. Obviously, the residual contents of Ni-CAT-1, CTF-1 and Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 are $54.23\ \%$ $29.69\%$ and $47.93\%$ respectively, when the temperature gradually increases to $800~^{\circ}C.$ . The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption experiments were performed to investigate the permanent porosity of Ni-CAT1, CTF-1 and Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 samples at 77 K. Ni-CAT-1, CTF-1 and Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 possess type IV isotherm with type H4 hysteresis loop, and their Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas are 281.1, 426 and $326.4\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively (Fig. 2d). Excellent pore structure and large surface area are favorable for the passage and adsorption of reactive molecules, which would promote the photocatalytic hydrogen production reaction.
The chemical state of as-prepared Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 was studied by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis. As shown in Fig. 3a, the characteristic peaks corresponding to C, N, O and Ni in the survey spectrum indicate that these elements exist on the surface of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1, which is consistent with the results of EDS, suggesting a successful assembly of Ni-CAT-1 and CTF-1.
The high-resolution XPS spectrum of C 1s is accurately deconvoluted into five characteristic peaks at 284.45, 285.02, 285.83, 286.83 and $288.70\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{C}{-}\mathsf{C},\mathsf{C}{-}\mathsf{N}$ in the triazine unit of CTF-1, CAO in Ni-CAT-1, $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ in the triazine unit of CTF-1 and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ in Ni-CAT-1, respectively (Fig. 3b) [47]. In the high-resolution XPS spectrum of N 1s (Fig. 3c), the prominent peak signal at 398.65 and $399.38\mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ and CAN in the triazine unit of CTF-1, while the weak signal at $398.50\ \mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to Ni-N, indicating the coordination bond connection between Ni-CAT-1 and CTF-1 in Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 hybrid material [48]. In Fig. 3d, the high-resolution XPS spectrum of Ni 2p exhibits two shakeup distinct peaks corresponding to the spin doublet of Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ apart from two satellite peaks, which confirms the existence of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ in Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 [49]. In order to further confirm the coordination bond connection, a comparison sample (CTF-1-Ni) was prepared by referring to the preparation procedure for Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 with the absence of organic-ligand (2,3,6,7,10,11-hexahydroxytriphenylene). As shown in the highresolution XPS spectrum of $\mathsf{N i2p}$ for CTF-1-Ni (Fig. S4a), the peaks at 875.22 and $857.09\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , implying that $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ can be adsorbed by CTF-1. Meanwhile, the highresolution XPS spectrum of N 1s is split into three peaks, corresponding to Ni-N, $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ and CAN, which verifies the inference of coordination bond connection between Ni-CAT-1 and CTF-1 in Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 hybrid material (Fig. S4b).
The ultraviolet–visible diffuse reflectance spectrum (DRS) and Mott-Schottky (MS) measurements were performed to determine the optical absorption property and band structure of samples. From Fig. 4a, Ni-CAT-1 sample exhibits whole ultraviolet–visible spectrum absorption, while CTF-1 possesses a narrow light region response. Due to the incorporation of Ni-CAT-1, the photoabsorption range of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 hybrid material is dramatically extended to up to $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The band gap $\left(\operatorname{E}_{\mathtt{g}}\right)$ of as-prepared CTF1 and Ni-CAT-1 was determined by Tauc’s plots [50], and the results are shown in Figure S5. Thus, the band gap of $2.62\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $1.38\ \mathrm{eV}$ was confirmed in CTF-1 and Ni-CAT-1 samples, respectively. Besides, the Mott-Schottky plots (Fig. 4b and Figure S6) of as-prepared samples were provided to determine the flat band potential $\left(\mathrm{E_{fb}}\right)$ of as-prepared samples. It is clear that CTF-1 and Ni-CAT-1 exhibit a positive slope, implying their $n$ -type semiconductor structure [36]. For $n$ -type semiconductors, the conduction band potential $(\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{C B}})$ is more negative about $_{0.1\mathrm{~V~}}$ relative to its $\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{f b}}$ [51]. Moreover, the valence band potential $(\mathrm{E}_{\mathsf{V B}})$ is the sum of $\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{C B}}$ and $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}.$ Therefore, the band structure of as-prepared samples can be determined. The $\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{C B}}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{VB}}$ of CTF-1 are $-0.83$ and 1.79 V (NHE), while The $\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{C B}}$ and $\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{V B}}$ of Ni-CAT-1 are $-1.29$ and 0.09 V (NHE), respectively.
The photocatalytic performance of as-obtained catalysts was evaluated by photocatalytic hydrogen evolution experiments under visible light irradiation $\left(>400\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ with sodium ascorbate as a sacrificial reagent. As a result, the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of parent CTF-1 is $1.96~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , while no detectable $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution was observed for Ni-CAT-1 (Fig. 4c). When Ni-CAT-1 was hybridized with CTF-1, a significant improvement in hydrogen evolution was observed. With the increase of Ni-CAT-1 amount $(1~\mathrm{wt\%}~-~9~\$ wt $\%)$ , the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of the as-prepared Ni-CAT-1/CTF1hybrid materials shows a trend of first increasing and then decreasing. This is mainly because the single Ni-CAT-1has no activity for photocatalytic hydrogen production. The excessive addition of Ni-CAT-1 will significantly reduce the content of the host catalyst (CTF-1), thus affecting the effect of photocatalytic hydrogen production. When the Ni-CAT-1 amount achieves $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ , the highest $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of $8.03~\mathrm{{mmol}}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ was obtained. Besides, a comparison of photocatalytic performance with recent related catalysts is provided in Table S2, which exposes the clear advantage of as-obtained Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 in photocatalytic hydrogen evolution. In order to further confirm the significant role of heterostructure between Ni-CAT-1and CTF-1 in enhancing the photocatalytic performance, the physically mixed sample (Ni-CAT-1-CTF-1) was prepared in our work. From Fig. 4d, the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rate $(2.82{\mathrm{~mmol~g}}^{-1}{\mathrm{h}}^{-1};$ of as-prepared Ni-CAT-1-CTF-1 is significantly lower than that of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1, which is attributed to the fact that the interactive interface formed in Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 greatly accelerates the transfer of photogenerated charges. The catalytic stability of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 was evaluated by five continuous photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution experiments $(25\ \mathrm{h})$ (Fig. 4e). The results show that Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 can maintain a stable catalytic rate during five consecutive testing cycles. In addition, in order to judge whether the photocatalytic material can maintain a certain structure after the hydrogen production reaction, the XRD characterization of the photocatalyst after the hydrogen production reaction was carried out. It can be concluded from Fig. 4f that after the reaction, the diffraction peaks CTF-1 are clearly observed in the composite while the diffraction peak corresponding to the (010) crystal plane in the Ni-CAT-1 decreases to some extent, but still exists, which indicates that the Ni-CAT-1has not been completely destroyed, and the hybrid material has a certain structural stability.
In order to explore the photocatalytic hydrogen evolution mechanism of as-prepared Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 hybrid material, the optical and electrical measurements were performed in this work. The charge migration ability of as-prepared samples was investigated by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), transient photocurrent measurement and photoluminescence (PL) emission spectroscopy. The EIS Nyquist plots are shown in Fig. 5a and Figure S7a, manifesting that Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 exhibits a smaller arc radius relative to CTF-1. Therefore, the introduction of Ni-CAT-1 has lowered the transmission resistance of electronics in the interface, which contributes to the improvement of charge transfer efficiency. As exhibited in Fig. 5b and Figure S7b, the transient photocurrent density of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 composite is about 2.5 times higher than that of CTF-1, implying that the photoexcited carriers realize faster separation/migration under visible light irradiation. The PL spectra of as-prepared samples are shown in Fig. 5c and Figure S7c. As a result, in comparison with CTF-1, the PL intensity of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 hybrid material is significantly quenched, which suggests a rapid charge transfer in Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1. From Fig. 5d and Figure S7d, the time-resolved PL decay spectra of asprepared samples show that the fluorescence lifetime of Ni-CAT1/CTF-1 $\mathit{\check{\Psi}}_{1}~=~0.95$ ns) is shorter relative to that of CTF-1 $(\uptau_{1}=2.34~\mathrm{ns})$ , indicating that Ni-CAT-1 can accept the electrons which are transferred from the CTF-1 through an additional nonradiative decay channel [52].
The transfer path of photogenerated electrons on the interface of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 was revealed for further studying the photocatalytic mechanism by the high resolution XPS spectroscopy. The electron flow direction can be determined by comparing the N 1s high resolution XPS spectra of CTF-1 and Ni-CAT-1 (Fig. 6a). In detail, the characteristic peaks of CTF-1 sample at $398.73\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $399.52\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ and $\mathsf{C-N}$ , respectively. An offset towards lower energy region is observed in NiCAT-1/CTF-1 composite relative to CTF-1, which is attributed to the electron cloud density of CTF-1 in the composite increasing, implying a definite flow direction of the photogenerated electrons in Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 composite from CTF-1 to Ni-CAT-1. Based on the above experiments and analyses, we proposed an internal mechanism of Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 in photocatalytic hydrogen evolution (Fig. 6b). Above all, CTF-1 is excited under visible light irradiation. The photogenerated electrons transfer from the VB to CB in CTF-1, and then quickly migrate to the Ni-CAT-1 through the coordination bond between CTF-1 and Ni-CAT-1 in the hybrid material to achieve the purpose that the photogenerated electrons and holes are isolated effectively. Similarly, the electrons on the VB of Ni-CAT-1 are also excited and migrate to its CB. Since the CB of Ni-CAT-1 is higher than that of CTF-1, the photogenerated electrons on the CB in CTF-1 transfer to the VB of Ni-CAT-1 and recombine with the holes on the VB. Therefore, the charge carrier transfer in Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 composites follows the Z-Sch eme electron-transfer mechanism. Due to the excellent electrical conductivity of Ni-CAT-1, the photogenerated electrons are concentrated and move relatively freely in the space occupied by Ni-CAT-1. Moreover, the porous structure of Ni-CAT-1 allows free passage of water molecules, resulting in the formation of an interactive micro-space on the surface of CTF-1 for electrons and hydrogen protons. Thus, the electrons which do not recombine with holes participate in $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ reduction in the presence of the Pt cocatalyst. Meanwhile, sodium ascorbate (SA) traps the holes left on the VB of CTF-1.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we proposed a novel integration mode for constructing tightly integrated MOF/COF heterostructures. In the previous report, the integration modes of MOF and COF components were usually limited to covalent connection [33–35]. In the present study, the coordinating connection was employed to construct the tightly integrated MOF/COF hybrid materials. In detail, the terminal amidine groups on the CTF-1 layers offer dual N sites for the coordination of metal ions, which provides the potential of coordinating connection of CTF-1 and Ni-CAT-1. As a result, a unique ZScheme heterostructure is formed between Ni-CAT-1 and CTF-1 components, and the tightly integrated Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1hybrid materials show significantly enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity. In particular, Ni-CAT-1/CTF-1 (1:19) sample exhibits a hydrogen evolution rate of $8.03\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ which is about four times higher than that of the parent CTF-1 $\mathrm{'1.96~mmol~g^{-1}h^{-1}})$ . The enhanced photocatalytic activity of NiCAT-1/CTF-1hybrid materials is attributed to the fact that the tightly coordinating connection dramatically lowers the barrier to charge transfer in the interface of Ni-CAT-1/CTF1heterostructures, and facilitates the separation of photogenerated carriers. The strategy in this work provides a new thought for constructing tightly integrated MOF/COF hybrid materials. In this way, more and more efficient photocatalysts with excellent charge separation performance will be exposed for hydrogen production.
